From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 06/21] dndcp: Add a clipboard client for ext-data-control-v1

A client that uses the core wl_data_device protocol can set or read the
selection only while it has keyboard focus, and the vmusr daemon has no
window to be focused. ext-data-control-v1 lets a client own and read the
selection without focus. KWin and wlroots compositors such as sway
implement it. GNOME does not.

waylandClipboard/ keeps its own connection to WAYLAND_DISPLAY and drives
it from the GLib main loop, because GDK stays on its X11 backend for the
drag and drop detection window. It binds the data control manager and a
device for the seat. WaylandClipboard_Offer() takes the selection with a
source that offers several MIME types and answers each request with the
bytes for the type requested. One selection can then carry rich text
next to its plain rendering, as the X11 backend does with several
selection targets. WaylandClipboard_Receive() reads one type of the
current selection and gives up at a deadline or past
CPCLIPITEM_MAX_SIZE_V3, the largest item the host accepts. The module
counts the selection events it sees, so that a caller can tell whether
the selection changed since it last read it, and it reports whether it
owns the selection itself.

Both directions use non-blocking pipes. The main loop also carries the
RPC channel, and a peer that stops reading or writing must not stall it.
A read stops at its deadline, and the sends draw on one time allowance
that refills at a tenth of the rate of wall clock time. The list of
types a peer offers is bounded.

The protocol code is generated from ext-data-control-v1.xml in
wayland-protocols with wayland-scanner when the plugin is built. The
next patch uses the module.
